Here is a sample JSON schema for a Beta Cruiser that includes at least one marker represented by square brackets:
```
{
  "$schema": "http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#",
  "title": "Beta Cruiser",
  "type": "object",
  "properties": {
    "name": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "The name of the cruiser"
    },
    "markers": {
      "type": "array",
      "items": {
        "type": "string",
        "pattern": "^\\[.*\\]$",
        "description": "A marker represented by square brackets"
      }
    }
  },
  "required": ["name", "markers"]
}
```
In this schema, we define a JSON object with two properties: `name` and `markers`. The `name` property is a string, and the `markers` property is an array of strings. Each string in the `markers` array must match the pattern `^\\[.*\\]$`, which means it must start with a square bracket, contain any number of characters, and end with a square bracket.

The `$schema` property specifies the version of the JSON schema used, in this case draft-07. The `title` property provides a human-readable name for the schema. The `type` property specifies that the root object is an object. The `properties` property defines the properties of the object, and the `required` property specifies that both `name` and `markers` are required.

